        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(e2e): bound non-root installer fetch",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe non-root install-sh Docker smoke runner fetches the official installer and pipes it straight into bash. Current `origin/main` bounds that pipe with `--connect-timeout 30 --max-time 300`, but it still streams the payload directly into the shell and uses a much looser budget than the sibling e2e runner:\n\n```sh\n# scripts/docker/install-sh-nonroot/run.sh (current origin/main)\ncurl -fsSL --connect-timeout 30 --max-time 300 -- \"$INSTALL_URL\" | bash\n```\n\nThis PR brings the non-root runner to the same **download-then-execute** contract as the sibling e2e runner merged in #108810 (`scripts/docker/install-sh-e2e/run.sh`): download the installer to a temp file with explicit connect/total timeouts, verify curl completed, then execute it, then clean up.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- **Fail fast on stall:** `--connect-timeout 10 --max-time 120` matches the values already merged for the sibling installer/download fixes in this area (#108810, #108911). A stalled fetch fails the step in under 2 minutes instead of tying up the runner for 5.\n- **No partial-stream execution:** with the temp-file form, bash only ever runs a fully downloaded installer; curl's exit status gates execution (`set -euo pipefail`).\n- **Consistency:** both Docker install-sh lanes now share the same fetch contract.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo user-visible behavior change to the installer itself. This only affects the non-root install-sh Docker CI lane: a stalled installer download fails fast with a clear curl error instead of streaming into a long-hanging pipe. Identical install semantics on success.\n\n## Evidence\n\nAfter (this PR, `scripts/docker/install-sh-nonroot/run.sh`):\n\n```sh\necho \"==> Run installer (non-root user)\"\n# Download the installer to a temp file first so a stalled connection or hung\n# response body cannot wedge the container run forever. Piping curl straight to\n# bash inherits curl's default of no total timeout.\ninstaller=\"$(mktemp)\"\ntrap 'rm -f \"$installer\"' EXIT\ncurl -fsSL --connect-timeout 10 --max-time 120 -o \"$installer\" \"$INSTALL_URL\"\nbash \"$installer\"\n```\n\nRegression tests (final head `15ad709b`, rebased onto latest main):\n\n```bash\npnpm vitest run test/scripts/test-install-sh-docker.test.ts\n```\n\n```text\nTest Files  1 passed (1)\n     Tests  77 passed (77)\n```\n\nCoverage includes a controlled stalled-transfer probe: the bounded fetch aborts on stall (the exact failure mode this change defends against), and the temp installer file is cleaned up via the EXIT trap. The runner-assertion test now checks the bounded temp-file form (`--connect-timeout 10 --max-time 120 -o \"$installer\"` + `bash \"$installer\"`).\n\n## What was not tested\n\nA live successful non-root **Docker** smoke run against the real installer endpoint: this contributor environment has no container runtime, so no Docker transcript is attached. Behavior on the success path is unchanged apart from the fetch budget (curl flags and execution order are asserted by the suite above); the stall path is proven hermetically.\n\n## CI note\n\nThe previous head failed `checks-node-compact-small-1` because the branch's own runner assertion still expected the old piped form (`curl ... | bash`) that this PR replaces. The assertion is aligned with the temp-file fetch on the current head, and the branch is rebased onto latest main.\n",
        "branch": "fix/bound-nonroot-install-fetch",
